 "script": [
  {
   "beat": "hook",
   "text": "In sixteen thirty-seven, one flower bulb cost more than a house. A flower. Bulb."
  },
  {
   "beat": "hook",
   "text": "And no, I'm not making this up. They called it Tulip Mania."
  },
  {
   "beat": "setup",
   "text": "Here's the deal. Holland was rich, and tulips were brand new, and super fancy."
  },
  {
   "beat": "setup",
   "text": "The rarest ones had wild flame stripes. Everybody wanted one."
  },
  {
   "beat": "boom",
   "text": "So prices went crazy. One old list says a single bulb was swapped for four fat oxen, eight pigs, twelve sheep, a ton of butter, and a bed."
  },
  {
   "beat": "boom",
   "text": "People traded them in taverns, for bulbs still buried in the dirt. Just paper promises. They called it the wind trade."
  },
  {
   "beat": "boom",
   "text": "And legend says a sailor ate a priceless bulb, because he thought it was an onion."
  },
  {
   "beat": "twist",
   "text": "And you know what made those stripes so special? A virus. The flowers were sick."
  },
  {
   "beat": "crash",
   "text": "Then, one day in February, at an auction in Haarlem, nobody showed up to buy."
  },
  {
   "beat": "crash",
   "text": "Boom. Prices crashed in days."
  },
  {
   "beat": "crash",
   "text": "Now, historians say the madness got blown up later. Most folks didn't go broke."
  },
  {
   "beat": "crash",
   "text": "But it's still one of the first famous money bubbles in history."
  },
  {
   "beat": "end",
   "text": "You get it? I said, you get it? People paid a fortune for a sick flower."
  },
  {
   "beat": "end",
   "text": "That's real. That's about it, folks. Follow for more strange history. See ya!"
  }
 ],
 "science": [
  {
   "claim": "Tulip prices in the Dutch Republic peaked in winter 1636-37 and collapsed in early February 1637 after a bulb auction in Haarlem drew no buyers; trading happened in taverns ('colleges') with contracts for bulbs still in the ground (windhandel, 'wind trade'); the most prized 'broken' tulips with flame stripes (e.g. Semper Augustus) were caused by a virus (tulip breaking virus, spread by aphids), identified in the 20th century; Charles Mackay (1841) listed goods worth 2,500 florins swapped for one Viceroy bulb (incl. 4 fat oxen, 8 fat swine, 12 fat sheep, 2 tons of butter, a bed) and the sailor-and-onion story; modern historians (Anne Goldgar, Tulipmania, 2007) argue the mania and its ruin were exaggerated.",
   "working": "",
   "source": "Wikipedia, Tulip mania; Goldgar 2007; Mackay 1841; Britannica"
  }
 ],
